mac: let a key reach the application, and let one it refuses leave again.

Two faults of the macOS backend, in one patch because they rewrite the same few
lines of keyDown:. Both are the difference between a pugl plugin and a JUCE one
on this platform, which is where both were found - juce_NSViewComponentPeer_mac.mm
has the matching code for each, in one case with the matching comment.

1. A key the application did not take died in the view. keyDown: dispatched the
event and returned, so an embedded view - which is the host's first responder for
as long as its window has the focus - swallowed every key it was handed, wanted
or not. The host lost its own key commands and its on-screen keyboard while the
plugin was in front. PUGL_FAILURE from the event handler now means "not mine" and
the event goes on to [super keyDown:], which is Cocoa's way of passing one along.

The status answers "is this keystroke mine", not "did this handler act on it".
A view with a text field must claim a printable key even though the work is done
by the PUGL_TEXT event, because the unhandled path hands the key on instead of
calling interpretKeyEvents: - a key the application refused is not text it is
composing, and the responder it goes to will do its own interpreting.

2. A command chord never arrived at all. Such a chord is a "key equivalent", and
the system routes those its own way: it offers the event to the views of the key
window through performKeyEquivalent:, then to the main menu, and only what nobody
claimed reaches keyDown:. A view that does not answer the method therefore never
sees one the menu wants, which for a plugin embedded in a host means nearly all
of them. Reported against SQ8L as command+arrow doing nothing in Cubase on macOS
while the same chord works in a JUCE plugin there. The dispatch is the one
keyDown: already did, factored out, so a key equivalent and an ordinary press
reach the application identically and PUGL_FAILURE keeps its meaning in both.

Two things that routing forces:

Every view of the key window is offered the chord, whether it has the keyboard or
not, so answering from the background would take command keys out of the host's
hands while the user worked somewhere else. Only the first responder answers.

An event refused here is delivered a second time, to keyDown:, once the menu has
passed on it. Dispatching it again would show the application one press twice, so
the refused event is remembered - timestamp and hardware key code, unique to one
press between them - and keyDown: goes straight to its superclass when it sees
that event again. JUCE guards the same way, over a wider set of fields.
diff --git a/src/mac.h b/src/mac.h
index 305cf30..a7b412b 100644
--- a/src/mac.h
+++ b/src/mac.h
@@ -37,6 +37,12 @@ struct PuglInternalsImpl {
   PuglWindow*      window;
   uint32_t         mods;
   bool             mouseTracked;
+  // The last key equivalent performKeyEquivalent: refused, which the system delivers a
+  // second time to keyDown: once the menu has had its turn (identified by timestamp and
+  // hardware key code, which together are unique to one press).
+  double           keyEquivTime;
+  unsigned short   keyEquivCode;
+  bool             keyEquivValid;
 };
 
 #endif // PUGL_SRC_MAC_H
diff --git a/src/mac.m b/src/mac.m
index 977cd7e..c362830 100644
--- a/src/mac.m
+++ b/src/mac.m
@@ -664,12 +664,8 @@ handleCrossing(PuglWrapperView* view, NSEvent* event, const PuglEventType type)
   puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &scrollEvent);
 }
 
-- (void)keyDown:(NSEvent*)event
+- (PuglStatus)dispatchKeyPress:(NSEvent*)event
 {
-  if (puglview->hints[PUGL_IGNORE_KEY_REPEAT] && [event isARepeat]) {
-    return;
-  }
-
   const NSPoint   wloc  = [self eventLocation:event];
   const NSPoint   rloc  = [NSEvent mouseLocation];
   const PuglKey   spec  = keySymToSpecial(event);
@@ -692,13 +688,63 @@ handleCrossing(PuglWrapperView* view, NSEvent* event, const PuglEventType type)
 
   PuglEvent pressEvent;
   pressEvent.key = ev;
-  puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &pressEvent);
+  return puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &pressEvent);
+}
+
+- (void)keyDown:(NSEvent*)event
+{
+  if (puglview->hints[PUGL_IGNORE_KEY_REPEAT] && [event isARepeat]) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  // A key equivalent that performKeyEquivalent: refused arrives here a second time, once the
+  // menu has had its turn, and dispatching it again would show the application one press
+  // twice. It has seen this one: all that is left is to let the responder chain have it.
+  if (puglview->impl->keyEquivValid &&
+      puglview->impl->keyEquivTime == [event timestamp] &&
+      puglview->impl->keyEquivCode == [event keyCode]) {
+    puglview->impl->keyEquivValid = false;
+    [super keyDown:event];
+    return;
+  }
 
-  if (!spec) {
+  // PUGL_FAILURE means the application did not take the key. Hand it on, or it dies here:
+  // an embedded view is the host's first responder while its window has the focus, so
+  // swallowing every key costs the host its own shortcuts and its virtual keyboard.
+  if ([self dispatchKeyPress:event] == PUGL_FAILURE) {
+    [super keyDown:event];
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if (!keySymToSpecial(event)) {
     [self interpretKeyEvents:@[event]];
   }
 }
 
+// A chord with command held is a "key equivalent", which the system routes its own way: it
+// offers the event to the views of the key window through this method, then to the main menu,
+// and only what nobody claimed reaches keyDown:. A view that does not answer never sees one
+// the menu wants - and for a plugin embedded in a host, the menu bar wants nearly all of them.
+//
+// Answered only while the keyboard is actually ours. Every view of the key window is offered
+// the chord, focused or not, so a plugin that answered from the background would pull command
+// keys out of the host's hands while the user was working somewhere else entirely.
+- (BOOL)performKeyEquivalent:(NSEvent*)event
+{
+  if ([[self window] firstResponder] != self) {
+    return [super performKeyEquivalent:event];
+  }
+
+  if ([self dispatchKeyPress:event] != PUGL_FAILURE) {
+    return YES;
+  }
+
+  puglview->impl->keyEquivTime  = [event timestamp];
+  puglview->impl->keyEquivCode  = [event keyCode];
+  puglview->impl->keyEquivValid = true;
+  return [super performKeyEquivalent:event];
+}
+
 - (void)keyUp:(NSEvent*)event
 {
   const NSPoint   wloc  = [self eventLocation:event];
@@ -723,7 +769,9 @@ handleCrossing(PuglWrapperView* view, NSEvent* event, const PuglEventType type)
 
   PuglEvent releaseEvent;
   releaseEvent.key = ev;
-  puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &releaseEvent);
+  if (puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &releaseEvent) == PUGL_FAILURE) {
+    [super keyUp:event];  // as in keyDown:, and in pairs: a host that saw the press wants it
+  }
 }
 
 - (BOOL)hasMarkedText
